  "description": {
   "content": [
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "The following xsd:dateTimes should be equivalent:",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "String a = \"1970-01-01T00:00:00.2Z\";",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "String b = \"1970-01-01T00:00:00.200Z\";",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "according to ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "attrs": {
          "href": "http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#dateTime"
         },
         "type": "link"
        }
       ],
       "text": "http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#dateTime",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "However, Fedora treats these as different dates, namely that \"a\" is 198 milliseconds before \"b\". This is due to what sane people would call a bug in SimpleDateFormat, but some spec engineers beg to differ:",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "SimpleDateFormat formatter = new SimpleDateFormat();",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "formatter.setTimeZone(TimeZone.getTimeZone(\"UTC\"));",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "formatter.applyPattern(\"yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss.S\");",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "Date aDate = formatter.parse(a);",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "formatter.applyPattern(\"yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss.SSS\");",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "Date bDate = formatter.parse(b);",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "System.out.println(aDate.equals(bDate));",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "System.out.println(aDate.getTime() + \" vs. \" + bDate.getTime());",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "which prints out:",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "false",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "2 vs. 200",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "This is the closest bug report I could find: ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "attrs": {
          "href": "http://bugs.sun.com/bugdatabase/view_bug.do?bug_id=4516915"
         },
         "type": "link"
        }
       ],
       "text": "http://bugs.sun.com/bugdatabase/view_bug.do?bug_id=4516915",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "Again, sane people would say that .2 is two-tenths, not two-thousandths, but I digress...",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "As the various DateUtility methods all rely on SimpleDateFormat for parsing and formatting dates, Fedora can record the wrong datetime and moreover, clients that ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "type": "strong"
        }
       ],
       "text": "do",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "text": " parse datetimes correctly can't pass them back to Fedora. For example, if Fedora serializes a dsCreateDate of \"1970-01-01T00:00:00.200Z\", and a client retrieves and (correctly) parses that as \"1970-01-01T00:00:00.2Z\" and uses that in a subsequent purgeDatastream request as the startDT and endDT, the purgeDatastream request will fail, as Fedora reads the date as \"1970-01-01T00:00:00.002Z\".",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Of course, if you actually had another datastream version at \"1970-01-01T00:00:00.002Z\", you would have successfully purged the wrong version.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    }
   ],
