  "description": "<p>The following xsd:dateTimes should be equivalent:</p>\n\n<p>        String a = \"1970-01-01T00:00:00.2Z\";<br/>\n        String b = \"1970-01-01T00:00:00.200Z\";</p>\n\n<p>according to <a href=\"http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#dateTime\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#dateTime</a></p>\n\n<p>However, Fedora treats these as different dates, namely that \"a\" is 198 milliseconds before \"b\". This is due to what sane people would call a bug in SimpleDateFormat, but some spec engineers beg to differ:</p>\n\n<p>        SimpleDateFormat formatter = new SimpleDateFormat();<br/>\n        formatter.setTimeZone(TimeZone.getTimeZone(\"UTC\"));</p>\n\n<p>        formatter.applyPattern(\"yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss.S\");<br/>\n        Date aDate = formatter.parse(a);<br/>\n        formatter.applyPattern(\"yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss.SSS\");<br/>\n        Date bDate = formatter.parse(b);</p>\n\n<p>        System.out.println(aDate.equals(bDate));<br/>\n        System.out.println(aDate.getTime() + \" vs. \" + bDate.getTime());</p>\n\n<p>which prints out:<br/>\n        false<br/>\n        2 vs. 200</p>\n\n<p>This is the closest bug report I could find: <a href=\"http://bugs.sun.com/bugdatabase/view_bug.do?bug_id=4516915\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://bugs.sun.com/bugdatabase/view_bug.do?bug_id=4516915</a><br/>\nAgain, sane people would say that .2 is two-tenths, not two-thousandths, but I digress...</p>\n\n<p>As the various DateUtility methods all rely on SimpleDateFormat for parsing and formatting dates, Fedora can record the wrong datetime and moreover, clients that <b>do</b> parse datetimes correctly can't pass them back to Fedora. For example, if Fedora serializes a dsCreateDate of \"1970-01-01T00:00:00.200Z\", and a client retrieves and (correctly) parses that as \"1970-01-01T00:00:00.2Z\" and uses that in a subsequent purgeDatastream request as the startDT and endDT, the purgeDatastream request will fail, as Fedora reads the date as \"1970-01-01T00:00:00.002Z\".</p>\n\n<p>Of course, if you actually had another datastream version at \"1970-01-01T00:00:00.002Z\", you would have successfully purged the wrong version.</p>\n",
